// Every decorated codepoint and the ASCII it stands for, generated by
// scripts/gen-unicode-fold.py: the decompositions of Unicode 16.0.0 for the
// compatibility variants (Letterlike Symbols, Mathematical Alphanumerics,
// fullwidth, circled, superscripts, ligatures), the confusables data (UTS #39,
// https://www.unicode.org/Public/security/latest/confusables.txt) at version
// 17.0.0 for the glyphs borrowed from another script purely because they look
// Latin. The script prints both versions, so a regen that lands newer ones
// updates this comment too; coverage is frozen at them until it is rerun.
//
// The compatibility half is baked rather than decomposed through Qt at run
// time, so that a name folds the same way in every build: the OUTLINED LATIN
// alphabet (U+1CCD6.., Unicode 16.0) is exactly what a font generator emits and
// exactly what a distribution's older Qt has never heard of. A baked table can
// only ever be ahead of a Qt, never behind, since Unicode guarantees an
// existing decomposition is never changed.
//
// The lookalike half is filtered, and the script documents that; in short an
// entry is kept when the confusable target reduces to a single ASCII letter
// once its combining marks are dropped, and when RemoveOneAccent (the upstream
// accent table this runs beside) does not already map it. Scripts with a large
// living user base (Cyrillic, Greek, the Indic family, CJK, ...) are left out
// on purpose: their letters are real text, not decoration. Of Armenian, Arabic,
// Georgian and their neighbours only the letters are left out, since their
// digits are what the generators borrow ("۷" for "v").
//
// Both cases of a bicameral letter are here even though the confusables data
// lists only the one whose glyph looks Latin (Cherokee is there as "Ꭰ", never
// as "ꭰ"): the fold runs on the name as it was written, and PrepareSearchWords
// lowercases only afterwards, so either case can reach it.
struct FoldPair {
	char32_t from = 0;
	const char *to = nullptr;
};

